    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-342264",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14128659/assets/7afa339233c7e21eda233f5b67ccf8be9e05fb4ce9484c98eaf59eb790e651ab.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14128659/assets/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)",
